<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="diagnostics" title="Diagnostic codes">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <p>
        Every condition %product% reports carries a code: <code>AW</code> and exactly four
        digits. The message changes with the class being woven. The code does not. The digits
        also fix the range, and the range says which part of the system refused.
    </p>

    <chapter title="Which range you are in" id="ranges">

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Range</td>
                <td>Raised by</td>
                <td>When it appears</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="diagnostics-declaration.topic">AW1xxx</a></td>
                <td>Processor, engine and extensions</td>
                <td>At compile and weave time</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="diagnostics-environment.topic">AW2xxx</a></td>
                <td>Drivers, engine and processor</td>
                <td>Mostly once; a few per class</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">AW3xxx</a></td>
                <td>Engine and drivers</td>
                <td>While a run is set up, and per class</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><a href="diagnostics-verification.topic">AW4xxx</a></td>
                <td>Engine, every driver</td>
                <td>While a class is woven, or before or after</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <note>
            <p>
                A code with a colon in it, such as <code>acme:WRAP_NOT_REENTRANT</code>, belongs
                to a plugin rather than to this catalogue. The namespace before the colon names
                the plugin that owns it.
                <a href="plugins.topic">Extending the engine</a> covers what a plugin may report.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Reading one line of output" id="anatomy">
        <p>
            A diagnostic prints as one line for the code, the place and the message. Then one
            indented line per detail, and a last line for the remedy.
        </p>

        <code-block lang="console">
AW1043 com.acme.audit.LedgerWeave.onCharge(Ljava/math/BigDecimal;)V matched 0 positions in charge, and requires at least 1
    injection: onCharge
    point: INVOKE target=Gateway.send
  remedy: the point's own diagnostic above lists what was found instead. If the target
    legitimately varies — two library versions, say — declare @Group(name = "…", min = 1)
    across the alternatives instead of requiring each one
        </code-block>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The code">
                <p>
                    The one part that is stable across releases and across messages. Search on
                    this, not on the wording.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The place">
                <p>
                    One description, never three. A source file, line and column wins; failing
                    that the target class and method, then the weave class and its handler, then
                    <code>&lt;unknown location&gt;</code>. A report that named no place at all
                    prints none — the sample above reports no target, so its line skips straight
                    to the message.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The message">
                <p>
                    What happened here. Where the reporting site supplied none, the catalogue's
                    one-line summary stands in, and that summary names no class or position.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The details">
                <p>
                    Indented lines the reporting site added: the candidates it found, the locals
                    live at that position, the members it compared.
                    <a href="injection-points.topic">Injection points</a> covers what a candidate
                    listing is telling you.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The remedy">
                <p>
                    Written by the reporting site rather than by the code, and absent from the
                    output when the site offered none. Most remedies are the same fixed sentence
                    for every occurrence of their code; the class, the method and what was found
                    are in the message and the details above it, not here.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                The severity does not appear on this line — <code>Diagnostic.format()</code>
                never writes it. Under the Maven plugin, the log level each diagnostic was
                printed at is the severity; under the annotation processor, the compiler's own
                prefix for the kind of message carries it.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Severity" id="severity">
        <p>
            Severity belongs to the condition, and a reporting site may raise or lower it for a
            single report.
        </p>

        <deflist type="narrow">
            <def title="ERROR">
                <p>A declaration that cannot be honoured, or output that would be wrong.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="WARNING">
                <p>Honoured, and more often a mistake than an intention.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="INFO">
                <p>A decision the framework made that you would not otherwise see.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="DEBUG">
                <p>Detail outside the normal narrative of a build.</p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Whether an error stops you" id="stopping">
        <p>
            That is the driver's decision, not the code's.
            <a href="choose-a-driver.topic">Choose a driver</a> has all three.
        </p>

        <p>The four Maven goals differ in what they report and whether it can fail the build:</p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr>
                <td>Goal</td>
                <td>Diagnostics</td>
                <td>Fails on an error</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>weave</code></td>
                <td>Logged at their own severity</td>
                <td>Yes, unless <code>failOnError</code> is <code>false</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>weave-tests</code></td>
                <td>Logged at their own severity</td>
                <td>Yes, unless <code>failOnError</code> is <code>false</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>stubs</code></td>
                <td><code>ERROR</code> at error; the rest at warn</td>
                <td>Never</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>audit</code></td>
                <td>None reported</td>
                <td>Never</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p><a href="maven-goals.topic">Maven goals</a> has the <code>failOnError</code> parameter.</p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The weaving class loader">
                <p>
                    An error does not stop it by itself. <code>aether.weaver.onError</code>
                    decides what happens to a class whose handling threw, and defaults to
                    <code>fail</code>: it stops rather than letting that class through unwoven.
                    <code>report</code> keeps the class as it arrived and the run goes on.
                    <a href="configuration-keys.topic">Configuration keys</a> has the key.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The failure reaches the listener its caller supplied as <code>AW4090</code>
                    first, before the policy is read. Give that message and a class dump to the
                    <a href="%issues%">issue tracker</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The agent">
                <p>
                    Same policy, same default — but no caller-supplied listener exists. The agent
                    collects diagnostics into its own list and prints it once, while installing;
                    one added afterward, while an ordinary class loads, is never printed. Under
                    <code>onError=fail</code> the transformer instead prints three lines and a
                    stack trace directly.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where the remedy comes from" id="remedy">
        <p>
            Two places carry it, and they are not the same thing.
        </p>

        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="The remedy in your output">
                <p>
                    The reporting site's own advice for this occurrence, absent when it offered
                    none. Read it first, since the code and the message already told you what
                    failed.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="The range page">
                <p>
                    It carries the condition in general: every situation that raises the code, and
                    what each one asks you to change. Follow the range from the table above.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            Five numbers are held against reuse, and no build produces one: <code>AW1003</code>,
            <code>AW1085</code>, <code>AW1086</code>, <code>AW2403</code> and
            <code>AW4002</code>. There is nothing to do about them.
        </p>
    </chapter>

